---
// CUSTOMER-FACING COPY RULE: this page states what we hold and for how long, and
// never where our cost comes from. Nothing on it may be invented.
//
// Source of truth: docs/data-retention.md. Every factual claim here — each data
// category, each retention period, each "never stored" statement — is that
// document's, restated in plain language. No claim was added, softened, or
// dropped. Where the source states a policy whose wording matters, its wording
// is kept.
//
// Last-updated date: the source document carries no date of its own, so this is
// the date it last changed in git (git log -1 -- docs/data-retention.md).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const lastUpdated = '25 September 2026';

// docs/data-retention.md, "What is stored".
const stored = [
  { what: 'Email', where: 'PocketBase', sensitivity: 'Personal' },
  { what: 'Password hash', where: 'PocketBase', sensitivity: 'Sensitive, but not usable if leaked (hashed)' },
  { what: 'Google account link', where: 'PocketBase', sensitivity: 'Personal' },
  { what: 'Telegram ID', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Personal, pseudonymous' },
  { what: 'Wallet balance + ledger', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Financial' },
  { what: 'Top-up history (amounts, dates)', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Financial' },
  { what: 'Token usage per day', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Behavioural' },
  { what: 'API keys', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Credentials (hashed) — the plaintext is never stored' },
  { what: 'Reviews + edit history', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Opinion, published aggregate only' },
  { what: 'Sessions', where: 'Postgres', sensitivity: 'Contains IP hash and user agent' },
];

// docs/data-retention.md, "What is NOT stored".
const notStored = [
  { what: 'Customer prompts', why: 'We are a proxy, not a data processor. Logging prompts makes us one without consent.' },
  { what: 'Model completions', why: 'Same.' },
  { what: 'Raw IP addresses', why: 'Only a hash, for abuse correlation.' },
  { what: 'Card or payment credentials', why: 'Midtrans handles payment; we never see them.' },
  { what: 'Plaintext API keys', why: 'Shown once, then only the hash.' },
  { what: 'Midtrans server key', why: 'Not customer data, but never logged either.' },
];

// docs/data-retention.md, "Retention periods".
const retention = [
  { what: 'Ledger', keep: 'Forever', why: 'Financial record; it is the authoritative audit trail' },
  { what: 'Top-ups', keep: 'Forever', why: 'Financial; matches the ledger' },
  { what: 'Usage daily', keep: '24 months', why: 'Billing disputes, then aggregate only' },
  { what: 'Sessions (expired/revoked)', keep: '30 days', why: 'Tidy up, but keep recent for security review' },
  { what: 'Reviews', keep: 'Until deleted by user', why: 'Published aggregate; individual text is theirs' },
  { what: 'Review history', keep: 'Same as review', why: 'Needed to make an edit meaningful' },
  { what: 'link_codes', keep: 'Until used or expired + 24h', why: 'Then delete' },
  { what: 'Logs', keep: '30-90 days', why: 'Debugging window; not a database' },
  { what: 'Accounts (closed)', keep: 'Keep record, drop personal data', why: 'See below' },
];

// docs/data-retention.md, "Access and deletion requests".
const requests = [
  { request: 'See your data', how: 'Dashboard + bot: profile, balance, usage, keys' },
  { request: 'Export it', how: 'A machine-readable export (not yet specified)' },
  { request: 'Correct it', how: 'Edit profile; the ledger is immutable by design' },
  { request: 'Delete your account', how: 'The closure flow below, anonymising where possible' },
  { request: 'Delete a review', how: '/review withdraw — the bot path' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — apikita"
  description="What apikita stores, for how long, what it never stores, and where your prompts go."
>
  <article class="space-y-12">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        This page is the factual record of what this service holds about you, how
        long it holds it, and what it never holds. It is written to be read, not
        to be skimmed: if something here is a commitment, it is a commitment we
        enforce in code review.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-muted">Last updated: {lastUpdated}.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What we store</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        What we hold, where it lives, and how sensitive it is.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-3">
        {stored.map((row) => (
          <li class="text-ink-muted">
            <strong class="text-ink">{row.what}</strong> — held in {row.where}.{' '}
            {row.sensitivity}.
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What we never store</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        This is the most important list on the page.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-3">
        {notStored.map((row) => (
          <li class="text-ink-muted">
            <strong class="text-ink">{row.what}</strong> — {row.why}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        The prompts-and-completions rule is a product promise. Customers send
        prompts through a proxy precisely because it should be a pipe. Storing
        them changes the relationship and becomes a liability the moment a breach
        occurs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How long we keep it</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-3">
        {retention.map((row) => (
          <li class="text-ink-muted">
            <strong class="text-ink">{row.what}</strong> — {row.keep}.{' '}
            {row.why}.
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        The ledger is never deleted, even when a customer leaves. It is the
        record of money that moved. That is normal accounting, not a retention
        violation — but it means anonymisation, not deletion, is the right
        mechanism for a departing customer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Closing your account</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        There is no hard delete of an account. Anything holding money is
        restricted from deletion at the database level, and accounts are never
        hard-deleted. Closing an account means:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 max-w-2xl list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink-muted">
        <li>Your account is marked closed.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">All sessions are revoked immediately</strong> — you are out.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">All API keys are revoked immediately</strong> — no further spend.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">The ledger and top-ups are retained</strong> (financial record).</li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">What can be anonymised is anonymised</strong> —
          your email is replaced with a tombstone, the Telegram link is removed,
          and a review body is cleared if you ask for it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">The balance row stays at zero.</strong> A
          closed account with a balance is unresolved money, so we do not close
          an account until the balance is zero.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        Never close an account that still holds a balance. Our non-refundable
        policy covers an unwanted service; it does not let us keep funds for a
        service we are refusing to provide.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where your prompts go</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        Every prompt is forwarded to a provider in mainland China. That is the
        business model, and it is the single largest privacy obligation in the
        system — so it is stated here plainly, before you use the service:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink-muted">
        <li>Prompts are forwarded to a mainland-China provider.</li>
        <li>The provider's jurisdiction is mainland China.</li>
        <li>We do not retain your prompts.</li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">The provider's own retention applies.</strong>{' '}
          That is outside our control. We do not store prompts, but the upstream
          may. A customer cannot be told "we do not keep your data" without also
          being told that someone else might.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Requests about your data</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-3">
        {requests.map((row) => (
          <li class="text-ink-muted">
            <strong class="text-ink">{row.request}</strong> — {row.how}.
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-muted">
        The ledger is the one thing that cannot be deleted on request. We say so
        before you ask, because explaining it after the fact reads as evasive.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Security that follows from this</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink-muted">
        <li>Passwords are hashed (bcrypt, via PocketBase).</li>
        <li>API keys are hashed (SHA-256).</li>
        <li>Session tokens are hashed and delivered in an HttpOnly cookie.</li>
        <li>Backups are encrypted and held off-host.</li>
        <li>No secrets appear in logs.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
